rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# Range 4: the Abort/Retry/Ignore key test, the RAM disk driver, the interrupt-state helper, FAT
# entry get/set, the FAT-copy mode, cluster to sector, and the path parser (drive, directories,
# 8+3 names) with the directory selection built on it.

@0FD6 KEY_ARI_ANSWER
  The tail of the Abort/Retry/Ignore prompt ($0F9C): classifies the key in A. Entered with Z set
  (from $0FCE, FLAG_SOFT_IRQ = $20) it answers Abort at once.
  1. Z on entry, "A", ESC or code 3: Abort, B = 1.
  2. B = 2; reads EXOS variable $90: when it is 0 (German) "W" is Retry, else "R"; ENTER is
  Retry too.
  3. B = 3; "I" is Ignore.
  Entry: A = the key (upper case), Z = abort already.
  Exit: Z with B = 1 (Abort), 2 (Retry) or 3 (Ignore); NZ for any other key (B = 3).
  Changes: AF, B.
  Keeps: C, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .0FD6 B = 1: Abort
  .0FD8 Abort already (Z)?
  .0FD9 ESC?
  .0FDC Code 3?
  .0FDF B = 2: Retry
  .0FE0 Keeps the answer number
  .0FE2 Keeps the key
  .0FE3 Read EXOS variable $90 (the message language)
  .0FE9 A = its value
  .0FEA 0 (German)?
  .0FEC A = the key
  .0FEE No: English, Retry is "R"
  .0FF0 German: Retry is "W"

@0FF4 KEY_ARI_ENGLISH
  Part of KEY_ARI_ANSWER: the English Retry key "R".
  .0FF4 Retry ("R")?

@0FF6 KEY_ARI_REST
  Part of KEY_ARI_ANSWER: answers Retry for the language's key or ENTER, then Ignore for "I".
  .0FF6 Puts DE back
  .0FF7 B = 2
  .0FF8 Retry key: Z, B = 2
  .0FF9 ENTER?
  .0FFC B = 3: Ignore
  .0FFD "I"? Z for Ignore, NZ for any other key

@1005 RAMDISK_DRIVER
  The RAM disk's driver entry: its address ($D005) is what $01CA passes to $03DF with C = 1,
  as $01B2 passes FLOPPY_DRIVER with C = 4. The disk lives in RAM segments; the first one, kept
  at IY+$1D, holds the boot sector at $4000 and the list of the disk's segments at $4100, and
  stays in page 1 while the driver runs.
  1. Pages the RAM disk's segment into page 1 (A' = the caller's page-1 segment).
  2. Function $FF: create (B = segments) or remove (B = 0) the disk (RD_CREATE).
  3. No RAM disk (IY+$1D = 0): error $BE.
  4. Functions 0-2: nothing to do. 3: reads sector 0. 4: reads, 5 and 6: write B sectors from
  sector DE, IX being the 512-byte buffers (in the caller's page-1 segment's view). 7 and up:
  error $BD.
  Entry: A = the function; B = the sector count (or segment count); DE = the first sector;
  IX = the buffer; IY = EXDOS's variables.
  Exit: A = 0; B = the sectors transferred (1 for functions 0-2); page 1 = the RAM disk's segment.
  Error: A = $BE (no RAM disk), $BD (no such function), $BB (sector past the end, B = the sectors
  done), or RD_CREATE's.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  .1005 Keeps the function
  .1006 H = the caller's page-1 segment
  .1009 L = the RAM disk's segment (0: none)
  .100D Page 1 = the RAM disk's segment
  .1010 A' = the caller's page-1 segment; A = the function
  .1011 Function $FF?
  .1015 RAM disk present?
  .1017 No
  .1019 Function 0-2?
  .101B Yes: nothing to do
  .101D Not function 3
  .101F Function 3: sector 0 ...
  .1021 ... one sector

@1023 RD_CHECK_FUNC
  Part of RAMDISK_DRIVER: functions 3-6 go on to the transfer; 7 and up fail with $BD.
  .1023 A = function - 7 ($FD-$FF for 3-6)
  .1025 Function 3-6: transfer
  .1027 Error $BD: no such function

@102A RD_NO_DISK
  Part of RAMDISK_DRIVER: no RAM disk exists; error $BE.
  .102A Error $BE: no RAM disk

@102D RD_FUNC_NOP
  Part of RAMDISK_DRIVER: functions 0-2 need nothing on a RAM disk; returns A = 0, B = 1.
  .102D One unit of work done
  .102F A = 0: done

@1031 RD_TRANSFER
  Part of RAMDISK_DRIVER: sets up the sector transfer. L counts the sectors done; the direction
  (bit 1 of the function - 7: functions 5 and 6 write) is kept in F'.
  .1031 L = sectors done
  .1033 H = function - 7
  .1035 F' = Z for a read (3, 4), NZ for a write (5, 6)

@1038 RD_SECTOR_LOOP
  Part of RAMDISK_DRIVER, once per sector: locates sector DE (RD_LOCATE_SECTOR), stops with $BB
  past the end, disables interrupts (INT_DISABLE) and sets HL and DE for the copy: from the
  buffer at IX to the segment for a write, the other way for a read.
  .1038 DE' = the sector number ...
  .1039 ... (the alternate set does the copy)
  .103B B = its segment, DE = its offset there
  .103E Past the end: error $BB
  .1040 Interrupts off while page 0 is swapped
  .1043 Keeps the interrupt state
  .1044 HL = the buffer
  .1047 Keeps it
  .1048 A = the caller's page-1 segment; F = the direction
  .1049 A write: copy buffer to segment
  .104B A read: copy segment to buffer

@104C RD_COPY_SECTOR
  Part of RAMDISK_DRIVER: copies one 512-byte sector with the caller's segment in page 1 (where
  the buffer is) and the RAM disk's data segment in page 0, then puts both pages back, moves IX
  on by 512 and enables interrupts again if they were on.
  .104C Page 1 = the caller's segment (the buffer)
  .104E Keeps it in A' again
  .104F C = page 0's segment
  .1052 Page 0 = the sector's segment
  .1055 A = page 0's own segment
  .1056 One sector
  .105B Page 0 back
  .105D Page 1 = the RAM disk's segment again
  .1062 HL = the buffer ...
  .1063 ... plus 512
  .1065 IX = the next buffer
  .1068 The interrupt state
  .1069 Were interrupts off before?
  .106B No: enable them again

@106C RD_NEXT_SECTOR
  Part of RAMDISK_DRIVER: counts the sector done and goes on with the next one until B sectors
  are done; then A = 0.
  .106C The counting set
  .106D One more sector done
  .106E Next sector number
  .106F More to do?
  .1071 A = 0: done

@1073 RD_DONE
  Part of RAMDISK_DRIVER: returns B = the sectors transferred (A = 0, or $BB from
  RD_SECTOR_LOOP).
  .1073 The counting set
  .1074 B = the sectors done

@1076 RD_LOCATE_SECTOR
  Finds where a RAM disk sector lives: 32 sectors of 512 bytes fill each 16K segment, and the
  segment list at $4100 gives the segment of each run of 32.
  Entry: DE = the sector number; the RAM disk's segment in page 1.
  Exit: NC; A = 0; B = the segment holding the sector; DE = its offset in the segment.
  Error: Carry and A = $BB when DE is not below the total sector count ($4013).
  Changes: AF, B, DE, HL.
  Keeps: C, IX, IY.
  .1076 HL = the total sectors (the boot sector's word at +$13)
  .107A HL = total - sector - 1
  .107C Error $BB
  .107E Sector DE past the end?
  .1080 HL = sector x 2
  .1081 A = (sector x 2) low byte
  .1082 HL = sector x 8
  .1084 L = sector / 32: the segment list's index
  .1087 B = the segment
  .1088 A = (sector mod 32) x 2
  .108A D = the offset's high byte (512 bytes a sector)
  .108B A = 0
  .108C E = 0

@1FC5 INT_DISABLE
  Disables interrupts and says whether they were enabled. LD A,R copies IFF2 into P/V, but an
  interrupt accepted during the instruction can leave it 0, so a 0 is checked again: the word
  just below SP still holds the AF pushed at the start (A = 0) unless an interrupt's return
  address overwrote it.
  Entry: none.
  Exit: interrupts disabled; NC when they were enabled, carry when they were not.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .1FC5 A = 0, NC
  .1FC6 Leaves A = 0 in the word below SP
  .1FC8 P/V = IFF2
  .1FCB Were enabled: NC
  .1FCC Back to the word pushed at the start
  .1FCE A = its high byte
  .1FCF Not 0: an interrupt came in between, so they were enabled
  .1FD1 Carry: interrupts were disabled

@1FD3 FLOPPY_DRIVER
  The floppy driver's entry: its address ($DFD3) is what $01B2 passes to $03DF with C = 4, as
  $01CA passes RAMDISK_DRIVER. Keeps the function at IY-$12 and dispatches it: 0 and 1 to $1FEB,
  2 to $2013, 3 to $2032, 4-6 to $2231, 8 to $241D; others (7 among them) fail with $BD.
  Entry: A = the function.
  Error: A = $BD (no such function).
  .1FD3 Keeps the function
  .1FD8 Function 0 or 1?
  .1FDA Function 2?
  .1FDD Function 3?
  .1FE1 Function 4-6?
  .1FE5 Function 7?
  .1FE8 Error $BD: no such function

@108E RD_CREATE
  RAMDISK_DRIVER's function $FF, create or remove the RAM disk: B = 0 removes it; a new disk
  of B segments may be made only when none exists, else error $9D.
  .108E Remove (B = 0)?
  .1091 A disk already there?
  .1092 Error $9D: it exists

@1095 RD_OPEN_CHAN
  Part of RD_CREATE: opens a channel to "DISK:" ($0329), whose special functions give and take
  back segments; B = 0 goes on to RD_REMOVE.
  .1095 Keeps the segment count
  .1099 H = the DISK: channel
  .109D Could not open it
  .109E Remove?
  .10A1 The segment list (entry 0 at $4100)

@10A4 RD_ALLOC_LOOP
  Part of RD_CREATE, once per segment: asks the DISK: channel for a segment (special function
  $F0). The first one becomes the RAM disk's own (page 1, IY+$1D). On failure the segments got
  so far are given back (RD_FREE_LOOP) with error $F7.
  .10A4 A = the channel
  .10A6 Special function $F0: give a segment
  .10AA A = the segment
  .10AC Failed: give them back, error $F7
  .10AE The first segment?
  .10B1 Yes: page it in ...
  .10B3 ... as the RAM disk's segment

@10B6 RD_STORE_SEG
  Part of RD_CREATE: lists each segment at $4100 + n, then closes the channel and writes the
  boot sector: the 11-byte parameter template and the 11-byte volume text from $1132 at $400B
  and $4040, the root directory entries ((segments / 4 + 2) x 16) at $4011, the total sectors
  (segments x 32) at $4013, two sectors a cluster at $400D for 128 segments and more, and the
  sectors of one FAT (RD_SET_FAT_SIZE).
  .10B6 List entry n = the segment
  .10B8 L = the segments listed
  .10BA More to get?
  .10BC Keeps the list's end
  .10C0 A = the channel
  .10C3 A = the segment count
  .10C4 The boot sector's parameters ...
  .10C7 ... from the template
  .10CF The volume text
  .10D6 L = segments / 4 ...
  .10DB ... + 2
  .10DD HL x 16
  .10E2 Root directory entries
  .10E5 HL = segments x 32
  .10EC Total sectors
  .10EF HL = 3 x total - 1 ...
  .10F4 ... / 1024: the sectors of a 12-bit FAT, less one
  .10F8 128 segments or more?
  .10FB Yes: two sectors a cluster ...
  .1100 ... and half the FAT

@1102 RD_SET_FAT_SIZE
  Part of RD_CREATE: stores the sectors of one FAT ($4016) and returns A = 0.
  .1102 L = the FAT's sectors less one
  .1104 Plus one
  .1105 Sectors a FAT
  .1108 A = 0: done

@110A RD_REMOVE
  Part of RD_CREATE, B = 0: removes the RAM disk. With none, just closes the channel; else gives
  every listed segment but the first back to the DISK: channel (RD_FREE_LOOP), closes it and
  forgets the disk (IY+$1D = 0).
  .110A A RAM disk at all?
  .110D No: close the channel only
  .110F DE = the list's end
  .1113 A = 0: the result
  .1114 Never jumps: its operand is LD A,$F7 at $1115 (from $10AC)
  .1117 Keeps the result (0, or $F7 from $1115)

@1118 RD_FREE_LOOP
  Part of RD_CREATE: gives the listed segments back from the last to entry 1 (special function
  $F1), then goes on to RD_CLOSE_EXIT.
  .1118 Back to entry 0?
  .1119 Yes: done
  .111B Special function $F1: take a segment back
  .111D The previous entry
  .111E C = its segment
  .1120 A = the channel
  .1121 Keeps the list pointer

@1128 RD_CLOSE_NONE
  Part of RD_CREATE: no RAM disk to remove; keeps the result and closes the channel.
  .1128 Keeps the result

@1129 RD_CLOSE_EXIT
  Part of RD_CREATE: closes the DISK: channel and marks the RAM disk gone (IY+$1D = 0); returns
  the result kept on the stack.
  .1129 A = the channel
  .112C A = the result
  .112D No RAM disk now

@114D FAT_GET_BADCL
  Part of $1148 (read a FAT entry): the cluster number is past the disk's last; reports error $A3
  through $0ED5, again while it returns Z, then answers the end-of-chain value $FFFF.
  .114D Error $A3
  .114F NC for $0ED5
  .1150 Reports it
  .1153 Report again?

@1252 SHL4_HL
  Shifts HL left by 4 bits into A: A:HL = HL x 16.
  Entry: HL = the value.
  Exit: HL = the value x 16 (low 16 bits); A = the 4 bits shifted out (entry H's high nibble).
  Changes: AF, HL.
  Keeps: BC, DE, IX, IY.
  .1252 A = 0
  .1253 Bit 15 out ...
  .1254 ... into A, 4 times

@1157 FAT_GET_UNPACK
  Part of $1148: takes the 12-bit entry out of its two bytes at DE: the low 12 bits for an even
  cluster (NC), the high 12 bits for an odd one (carry, from $11ED).
  .1157 Keeps HL
  .1158 L = the first byte
  .115B A = the second byte
  .115C An even cluster?
  .115E Odd: H = the second byte
  .115F A = its high nibble
  .1162 L = the middle 8 bits

@1163 FAT_GET_CHECK
  Part of $1148: DE = the entry; a value above $FF7 (end of chain or bad) becomes $FFFF.
  .1163 The entry's top 4 bits
  .1166 DE = the entry
  .1167 Above $FF7?
  .116D No: NC, DE = the next cluster

@116E FAT_GET_END
  Part of $1148: answers DE = $FFFF, the end of the chain.
  .116E End of chain

@1172 CLUSTER_TO_SECT
  Gives the disk sector of a sector within a cluster: (cluster - 2) shifted left by the drive's
  sectors-a-cluster power, plus the sector, plus the first data sector.
  Entry: HL = the drive's parameter block (+$09 the first data sector, +$0B the power of 2 of the
  sectors a cluster); DE = the cluster; A = the sector within the cluster.
  Exit: DE = the sector number.
  Changes: AF, DE.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .1174 HL = the block + $0B
  .1178 B = the shift count ...
  .1179 ... + 1 for the DJNZ
  .117A HL = the cluster ...
  .117B ... - 2
  .117D Its operand byte ($29) is the loop's ADD HL,HL at $117E
  .117F Shifts HL left the count times
  .1181 Plus the sector in the cluster
  .1184 HL = the block + $09
  .1187 HL = the first data sector
  .118B Plus the cluster's sector
  .118C DE = the sector number

@125C FAT_SYNC_COPIES
  Ends the single-FAT mode of the drive whose parameter block is HL: when its +$18 bit 0 is set,
  marks every sector of the first FAT to be written to all the copies (bit 6 of each buffer's
  flags, through $0AD2/$0B8F), then sets +$18 to 0 and stores the 0 in the boot sector (data byte
  $46) too (FAT_MODE_SET).
  Entry: HL = the drive's parameter block.
  Exit: the mode cleared; IX as on entry.
  Changes: AF, HL, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, IX.
  .125C IX = the block (its own IX kept on the stack)
  .125F Single-FAT mode on?
  .1263 No: nothing to do
  .1265 A = 0: write to all copies, mode 0

@1190 FAT_SET
  Writes a cluster's FAT entry: first ends the single-FAT mode (FAT_SYNC_COPIES), then stores the
  12-bit value in the FAT's buffer ($1193 on, FAT_SET_STORE) and marks it to be written. A value
  of $1000 or more is stored as $FFF.
  Entry: HL = the drive's parameter block; DE = the cluster; BC = the value.
  Exit: the entry set in the buffer.
  Changes: AF, BC, HL, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: DE, IX.
  .1190 Writes go to every FAT copy again

@119C FAT_SET_LOCATE
  Part of FAT_SET: finds the entry's two bytes ($11ED); a cluster past the disk's end goes to
  FAT_SET_BADCL.
  .119C DE = the entry's bytes, carry for an odd cluster
  .119F Found

@11A1 FAT_SET_BADCL
  Part of FAT_SET: the cluster number is past the disk's last; reports error $A3 through $0ED5,
  again while it returns Z, and writes nothing.
  .11A1 Error $A3
  .11A3 NC for $0ED5
  .11A4 Reports it
  .11A7 Report again?

@11AB FAT_SET_STORE
  Part of FAT_SET: an even cluster takes the low 12 bits of the two bytes: C in the first, B's
  low nibble under the second byte's high nibble.
  .11AB Keeps HL
  .11AC An odd cluster?
  .11AE First byte = the value's low byte
  .11B1 Second byte: keeps its high nibble ...
  .11B4 ... and takes the value's top 4 bits

@11B8 FAT_SET_ODD
  Part of FAT_SET: an odd cluster takes the high 12 bits: the value x 16 goes in under the first
  byte's low nibble.
  .11B8 HL = the value
  .11BA HL = the value x 16
  .11BD First byte: keeps its low nibble ...
  .11C0 ... and takes the value's low 4 bits
  .11C3 Second byte = the value's top 8 bits

@11C5 FAT_SET_DIRTY
  Part of FAT_SET: marks the buffer to be written. When the entry straddles two sectors (IY+$63
  bit 0, its bytes kept at IY+$66), the second byte goes to the first data byte of the second
  sector's buffer and the first byte to the last of the first sector's ($11D9, the sector kept at
  IY+$64).
  .11C5 Mark the buffer changed (HL = its flags)
  .11C8 Did the entry straddle two sectors?
  .11CC No: done
  .11CE HL = the buffer's first data byte
  .11D2 The second byte goes there
  .11D3 A = the first byte
  .11D5 DE = IY+$64: the first sector (byte $64 follows)

@11D9 FAT_SET_SPLIT
  Part of FAT_SET (after the inline byte at $11D8): puts the first byte of a straddling entry in
  the last data byte of its sector's buffer.
  .11D9 IX = the parameter block (saved on the stack)
  .11DB Keeps the byte
  .11DC HL = the sector's buffer
  .11DF Mark it changed
  .11E2 HL = its last data byte
  .11E7 The first byte goes there
  .11E8 IX back

@11EA FAT_SET_EXIT
  Part of FAT_SET: puts HL back and returns through FAT_SET_RET.
  .11EA HL back

@11EB FAT_SET_RET
  Part of FAT_SET: puts the caller's DE back and returns.
  .11EB DE back (the cluster)

@1248 FAT_LOC_FOUND
  Part of $11ED (find a FAT entry): DE = the entry's two bytes; Z, with carry for an odd cluster
  (the half byte of cluster x 1.5).
  .1248 DE = the entry's bytes
  .1249 Carry = the odd half
  .124A A = 0 or $FF
  .124B Z: found
  .124C Carry = odd cluster

@124D FAT_LOC_EXIT
  Part of $11ED: puts HL, BC and IX back and returns (NZ, from $1202, when the cluster is past the
  last).
  .124D HL back

@1268 FAT_SINGLE_ON
  Starts the single-FAT mode of the drive whose parameter block is HL, unless its +$19 bit 7 is
  set or the mode is on already: goes over every sector of the first FAT through $0AE0 and
  $09F9 with one copy, then sets +$18 to 1 and stores the 1 in the boot sector (FAT_MODE_SET).
  From then on FAT writes go to the first copy only, until FAT_SYNC_COPIES.
  Entry: HL = the drive's parameter block.
  Exit: the mode on; IX as on entry.
  Changes: AF, HL, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, IX.
  .1268 IX = the block (its own IX kept on the stack)
  .126B Drive barred from single-FAT mode?
  .126F Yes: leave the mode alone
  .1271 Single-FAT mode on already?
  .1277 A = 1: one copy, mode 1

@1279 FAT_MODE_SET
  Part of FAT_SYNC_COPIES and FAT_SINGLE_ON (A = the new mode, 0 or 1): with more than one FAT,
  goes over the first FAT's sectors (FAT_MODE_LOOP); then records the mode (FAT_MODE_MARK).
  .1279 Keeps BC
  .127A Keeps DE
  .127B B = the number of FATs
  .127E Only one?
  .127F Yes: just record the mode
  .1281 DE = the first FAT sector
  .1287 B = the sectors of one FAT

@128A FAT_MODE_LOOP
  Part of FAT_MODE_SET, once per FAT sector: mode 1 takes its buffer through $0AE0 (one copy);
  mode 0 through $0AD2, then marks it changed and to be written to all copies (bit 6).
  .128A Keeps the sector count
  .128B Keeps the mode
  .128C Mode 1?
  .128D B = 0 for $0AE0
  .128F Mode 1: the buffer, one copy
  .1292 Mode 1
  .1294 Mode 0: the buffer, every copy
  .1298 Mark it changed (HL = its flags)
  .129B Write it to all the copies

@129E FAT_MODE_NEXT
  Part of FAT_MODE_SET: passes the buffer to $09F9, clears its byte +2 and goes on with the next
  FAT sector; then B = the mode.
  .12A1 HL = the buffer's byte +2
  .12A5 A = the mode
  .12A6 B = the sectors left
  .12A7 The next FAT sector
  .12AA B = the mode

@12AB FAT_MODE_MARK
  Part of FAT_MODE_SET: when the mode changes, records it in +$18 and in the boot sector (sector
  0's buffer, data byte $46).
  .12AB A = the new mode
  .12AC Is it the mode already?
  .12AF Yes: nothing to record
  .12B2 Sector 0 ...
  .12B5 ... one copy
  .12BD Mark it changed (HL = its flags)
  .12C0 HL = data byte $46
  .12C5 The mode in the boot sector
  .12C6 And in the parameter block

@12C9 FAT_MODE_POP
  Part of FAT_MODE_SET: puts DE and BC back.
  .12C9 DE back

@12CB FAT_MODE_EXIT
  Part of FAT_SYNC_COPIES, FAT_SINGLE_ON and FAT_MODE_SET: puts IX and HL back and returns.
  .12CB IX back; the parameter block on the stack
  .12CD HL = the parameter block

@1343 DIRPOS_PTRS
  Sets up the copy of a 12-byte directory position between a file's descriptor (from +$17) and
  the current one at IY+$30.
  Entry: IX = the descriptor.
  Exit: DE = IY+$30; HL = IX+$17; BC = 12.
  Changes: F, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: A, IX, IY.
  .1346 DE = IY+$30: the current position
  .134E HL = the descriptor's position
  .1352 12 bytes

@1326 DIRPOS_RESTORE
  Makes the directory position kept in a descriptor the current one: selects its drive ($0CC9)
  and copies its 12 bytes from +$17 to IY+$30. Sets IY+$63 bit 2 either way (the sector buffer
  must be found again).
  Entry: DE = the descriptor (+$07 the drive).
  Exit: Z, A = 0; IX = the descriptor; the position copied.
  Error: NZ, A = the error from $0CC9.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: BC.
  .1327 IX = the descriptor
  .132A C = its drive
  .132F Select the drive
  .1332 Failed?
  .1336 Set up the copy
  .1339 The position is current
  .133C A = 0: done

@133D DIRPOS_EXIT
  Part of DIRPOS_RESTORE: puts BC back and marks the buffer to be found again (IY+$63 bit 2).
  .133D BC back
  .133E The entry's buffer must be found again

@16C7 DIR_REFETCH
  Gives the current directory entry again after the position was restored: counts one more entry
  left in the sector (IY+$39) and takes a step of 0 bytes through the stepping code at $16D4,
  which comes back with DE = the entry in its sector buffer.
  Entry: DE = the current entry; IY+$30 on, the directory position.
  Exit: DE = the entry (from $16D4 on).
  .16C7 Undoes the step's count of one entry
  .16CA Keeps BC
  .16CB A step of 0 bytes ($16D0's is $20)

@12F9 DIR_REOPEN_ENTRY
  Goes back to the directory entry of an open file: the descriptor at DE must start with $FE;
  the position kept in the block at HL is restored (DIRPOS_RESTORE), the 4 bytes at the
  descriptor's +$19 are copied to +$2C, and the entry is fetched again (DIR_REFETCH).
  Entry: DE = the file's descriptor (first byte $FE); HL = the block with the position.
  Exit: Z; A = the entry's attributes (its byte $0B); DE = the entry; IX = the descriptor.
  Error: NZ, A = $A0 (not a $FE descriptor) or DIRPOS_RESTORE's error.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: BC.
  .12F9 The descriptor's first byte
  .12FA $FE?
  .12FC Error $A0
  .12FF Keeps the descriptor
  .1300 DE = the position's block
  .1301 Restore the position
  .1305 Failed
  .1309 HL = the descriptor + $19 ...
  .130E ... DE = + $2C
  .1312 4 bytes
  .1316 HL = the descriptor
  .1317 DE = the directory entry
  .131B HL = its byte $0B ...
  .131F ... A = the attributes
  .1321 IX = the descriptor
  .1324 Z: done

@1646 DIR_ENTER_ENTRY
  Makes the directory whose entry DE points at the one to search: its start cluster (the entry's
  word at +$1A) goes to IY+$30; cluster 0 means the root ($FF00). With C' bit 2 set, a start
  cluster equal to IY+$5E sets IY+$5D bit 6 ($15A8 on).
  Entry: DE = the directory entry.
  Exit: Z, A = 0; IY+$30 = the directory's start cluster.
  Changes: AF, DE.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .1646 Keeps HL (popped at $15C4)
  .1647 HL = the entry's start cluster
  .164E 0 (the root)?
  .1650 No: that cluster
  .1653 Yes: the root

@12CF DIR_SELECT
  Selects the directory a search runs in, and starts it at its first entry (DIR_FIRST):
  1. DE's first byte $FF: the directory is the path text at HL (DIR_SELECT_PATH).
  2. Else DE is an open file's descriptor: its entry is fetched again (DIR_REOPEN_ENTRY), must be
  a directory (attribute bit 4) and becomes the one to search.
  Entry: DE = the descriptor or $FF; HL = the path text or the block with the position; B, C as
  PATH_PARSE takes them.
  Exit: Z; the search started.
  Error: NZ, A = $A1 (not a directory), $A8 (the path text does not end the path) or the error of
  DIR_REOPEN_ENTRY or PATH_PARSE.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  .12CF The first byte
  .12D0 $FF?
  .12D1 Yes: a path
  .12D3 A = the entry's attributes
  .12D6 Failed
  .12D7 A directory?
  .12DB Error $A1: not a directory
  .12DE Search this directory

@1513 PATH_GET_CHAR
  Reads the next character of the path text at DE' (the alternate set): from segment IY+$2D
  paged into page 1 unless C' bit 3 is set (the text is in view). Unless C' bit 1 is set, a-z
  become upper case and "!" and "'" are taken as "\".
  Entry: DE' = the text; C' = the flags.
  Exit: NZ, A = the character, DE' past it; Z, A = 0 at the end (DE' stays).
  Changes: AF, DE'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .1513 The text's set
  .1515 Text in view?
  .1519 Keeps BC'
  .151C B = page 1's segment
  .151E Page 1 = the text's segment
  .1523 A = the character
  .1524 Page 1 back
  .1526 BC' back

@14DB IS_CAPITAL
  Tests for a capital letter.
  Entry: A = the character.
  Exit: Z (NC) for "A"-"Z"; NZ otherwise, with carry below "A".
  Changes: F.
  Keeps: A, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .14DB Below "A"?
  .14DE Above "Z"?
  .14E2 Z: a letter

@1546 PATH_UNGET_CHAR
  Steps the path text back by one character: DE' - 1.
  Entry: DE' = the text.
  Exit: DE' = the text - 1.
  Changes: DE'.
  Keeps: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .1547 Back one character

@1369 PATH_PARSE
  Reads a path: "[drive:][\]dir\...\name.ext". Swaps the register sets (the caller's are the
  alternates while it runs, the text read through DE') and leaves them swapped. The flags are
  the caller's C.
  1. A letter and ":" give the drive (1 = A); else IX+7, or the default drive IY-$57 when that
  is 0. IX+7 = the drive.
  2. Flag bit 7: selects the drive ($0CC9, HL = its parameter block) and sets the start
  directory (PATH_START_DIR): the root after a "\" (or with IX+6 bit 3), else the drive's
  current one.
  3. IX+6 bit 3: the rest is one raw 11-character name up to "/" (PATH_RAW_NAME). Else each
  element is read into the name buffer (PARSE_NAME_ELEM) and, when a "\" follows, looked up and
  entered (PATH_FIND_ELEM); the last one too when flag bit 0 is set and it has a name.
  4. PATH_FINISH writes 0 at the text position kept at IY+$5B (where the last directory element
  ended) and, with flag bit 7, clears the word at the caller's HL.
  Entry: DE = the text; C = the flags; HL = the word to clear; IX = the parse block (+6 flags,
  +7 drive or 0).
  Exit: the sets swapped; Z, A = 0; DE = the text after the path; C = the character that stopped
  it (0 at the end); B = the last element's flags; the name buffer (IY+$6D1) holds it.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code ($0CC9's, or a lookup's).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  .1369 Text not in view ...
  .136B ... and folded to upper case
  .136D The text goes to the alternate set
  .136E B = flags: bit 2, a drive was given
  .1370 Empty?
  .1375 A letter?
  .1378 No: no drive
  .137A C = the drive number (1 = A)
  .137D Anything after it?
  .1382 ":"?
  .1384 Yes: a drive
  .1386 No: back to the letter

@1389 PATH_UNGET1
  Part of PATH_PARSE: steps the text back by one character, then takes the default drive.
  .1389 Back to the start

@138C PATH_DEF_DRIVE
  Part of PATH_PARSE: no drive in the text: the parse block's drive, or the default (IY-$57) when
  that is 0.
  .138C A = the block's drive
  .138F C = the default drive
  .1392 None in the block?

@1396 PATH_NO_DRIVE
  Part of PATH_PARSE: no drive was given (B bit 2 cleared).
  .1396 No drive given

@1398 PATH_SET_DRIVE
  Part of PATH_PARSE: stores the drive in the block, selects it when C' bit 7 is set ($0CC9),
  and reads a leading "\" (B bits 0 and 1); IX+6 bit 3 forces the root.
  .1398 The block's drive
  .139B Keeps the flags
  .139D B = 0 for $0CC9
  .139F Look the path up?
  .13A2 Yes: select the drive
  .13A6 Failed?
  .13A8 A raw name (from the root)?
  .13AC Keeps the answer
  .13AF The first character
  .13B2 End of text
  .13B4 "\"?
  .13B6 No: put it back
  .13B8 Yes: B bit 0, a "\" was read

@13BA PATH_FROM_ROOT
  Part of PATH_PARSE: B bit 1, start at the root; A = 0 so nothing is put back.
  .13BA Start at the root

@154A PATH_START_DIR
  Sets the directory a path starts in (IY+$30): the root when B bit 1 is set or the drive has no
  current directory (+$1E bit 7 of its parameter block); else walks the drive's current-directory
  path, kept as text at +$20, from the root through PARSE_NAME_ELEM and $15D4. When that walk
  fails, the path text is cleared (+$20 = 0) and the root is used.
  Entry: HL = the drive's parameter block; B = the flags; the path text at DE' (C' its flags).
  Exit: A = 0; IY+$30 = the start directory's cluster.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', HL'.
  Keeps: BC.
  .154A Keeps the parameter block
  .154B From the root?
  .154F HL = the current directory's cluster ...
  .1556 None (bit 7)?
  .155A Z when it is cluster 0
  .155C HL = the block; its +$1F on the stack
  .155D The text's set
  .155E HL = the block + $1F
  .1560 Keeps the caller's flags and text
  .1562 DE = the kept path text (+$20)
  .1564 Text in view
  .1566 Look each element up ...
  .156A ... unless the start cluster is 0

@13BD PATH_START
  Part of PATH_PARSE: puts a non-"\" character back, sets the start directory when C' bit 7 is
  set (PATH_START_DIR), keeps the text position at IY+$5B and goes on with the raw name
  (PATH_RAW_NAME) or the elements (PATH_ELEM_LOOP).
  .13BD Not a "\": put it back
  .13C1 Look the path up?
  .13C4 Yes: set the start directory
  .13C8 Keeps the text position
  .13CF The raw-name answer
  .13D0 A raw name

@1666 NAME_BUFFER
  Gives the 11-byte name buffer (8 + 3 characters).
  Entry: IY = EXDOS's variables.
  Exit: HL = IY+$6D1.
  Changes: F, HL.
  Keeps: A, BC, DE, IX, IY.
  .166A HL = IY+$6D1

@14E6 IS_BAD_NAME_CHR
  Tests whether a character may not be part of a file name: a control code, or one of the 19 in
  the table at $1500 (space, tab and : ; . , = + # \ ! ' < > | / " [ ]).
  Entry: A = the character.
  Exit: Z when it may not; NZ when it may.
  Changes: F.
  Keeps: A, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .14E6 A control code?
  .14E8 No: search the table
  .14EA Z: not allowed

@143E PARSE_NAME_ELEM
  Reads one path element into the name buffer (NAME_BUFFER) as 8 + 3 characters padded with
  spaces: "." and ".." are taken whole; else the name part up to a "." or a character not
  allowed in a name, then the extension after a ".".
  Entry: the text at DE' (C' its flags); B = the flags (bits 0-2 kept).
  Exit: A = the character that stopped it (unread; 0 at the end); B bit 0 = something stored,
  bit 3 = a name part, bit 4 = a "." before an extension, bit 5 = wildcards, bit 6 = "." or
  "..", bit 7 = "..".
  Changes: AF, BC, DE'.
  Keeps: DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .1440 HL = the name buffer
  .1443 8 characters of name
  .1445 Keeps the low 3 flags
  .1449 The first character
  .144C End: an empty name
  .144E "."?
  .1450 No: put it back, read a name
  .1452 One dot
  .1454 The next one
  .1457 End: "."
  .1459 Allowed in a name?
  .145C Yes: put both back, read a name
  .145E A second "."?
  .1460 No: put it back, "."
  .1462 ".."
  .1464 Two dots
  .1465 The next one
  .1468 End: ".."
  .146A Allowed in a name?
  .146D Yes: put them back, read a name
  .146F A third "."?
  .1471 Yes: put them back, read a name

@15C6 PATH_FIND_ELEM
  Remembers where an element ended (IY+$5B) and marks B bit 1; when C' bit 7 is set, looks the
  element up in the directory being searched and enters it ($15D4 on).
  Entry: B = the element's flags; DE' = the text; C' = the flags.
  Exit: A = 0 (not looked up, or found).
  Error: A = $A8 or $AA from the lookup.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  .15C6 A = 0
  .15C7 B bit 1
  .15CA Look it up?
  .15CC Keeps the text position
  .15D3 No: done

@13D2 PATH_ELEM_LOOP
  Part of PATH_PARSE: reads each element; one followed by "\" is a directory, looked up and
  entered (PATH_FIND_ELEM), and the "\" is skipped.
  .13D2 Reads an element
  .13D5 A "\" after it?
  .13D7 No: the last element
  .13D9 Enter the directory
  .13DC Failed?
  .13DF Skips the "\"

@13E4 PATH_LAST_ELEM
  Part of PATH_PARSE: the last element is looked up too when C' bit 0 is set and it has a name or
  an extension (B bits 3, 4).
  .13E4 A = 0
  .13E6 Look the last element up?
  .13EB A name or a "."?
  .13EE Yes: look it up

@13F1 PATH_FINISH
  Part of PATH_PARSE: C = the character after the path (0 at the end); clears the word at the
  caller's HL when flag bit 7 is set, then PATH_CUT_TEXT.
  .13F1 Keeps the result
  .13F2 The next character
  .13F5 Not the end: put it back
  .13F8 C = it
  .13F9 A = the result
  .13FA The caller's set
  .13FD Look the path up?
  .1401 Yes: clear the word at HL

@1404 PATH_CUT_TEXT
  Part of PATH_PARSE: writes 0 at the address kept in IY+$5B, returns the text pointer in DE
  with the sets left swapped, and Z for A = 0.
  .1404 HL = the address kept
  .140A 0 there
  .140B DE = the text ...
