@14A5 COPY_NAME_PART
  Copies a name part from the path text to HL: at most C characters, upper case as
  PATH_GET_CHAR gives them; "*" fills the rest with "?"; characters past C are skipped; the rest
  is padded with spaces. Stops at the end or at a character not allowed in a name, which is put
  back.
  Entry: C = the part's length; HL = where it goes; the text at DE' (C' its flags); B = the
  flags.
  Exit: Z; HL past the part; D = 1 when a character was taken, else 0; B bit 0 set for one, bit
  5 for "?" or "*"; A = the character that stopped it (0 at the end).
  Changes: AF, BC, D, HL, DE'.
  Keeps: E, IX, IY.
  .14A5 Nothing taken yet

@148C NAME_MAIN_PART
  Part of PARSE_NAME_ELEM: copies the name part (8 characters); B bit 3 when it has one.
  .148C The name part
  .148F Anything taken?
  .1492 Yes: a name

@1494 NAME_EXT_DOT
  Part of PARSE_NAME_ELEM: a "." starts the extension (B bit 4) and is skipped.
  .1494 "."?
  .1498 Yes: an extension
  .149A Skips it

@149D NAME_EXT_PART
  Part of PARSE_NAME_ELEM: copies the extension (3 characters) and returns.
  .149D 3 characters
  .149F The extension

@16AA DIR_FIRST
  Starts a search of the directory whose start cluster is at IY+$30 (bit 7 of the high byte set:
  the root): DE = that cluster, then on at $16AE.
  Entry: IY+$30 = the directory's start cluster; HL = the drive's parameter block.
  Exit: as $16AE on.
  .16AA DE = IY+$30 (byte $30 follows)

@12E3 DIR_SELECT_PATH
  Part of DIR_SELECT: reads the path text (PATH_PARSE with flags $81: look it up, the last
  element too); anything left after the path is error $A8.
